In this section, we study several examples in topology.
First of all, if $X$ is a topological space and $Y$ is a nonempty subset of $X$,
one can give a canonical topology on $Y$ using the one of $X$.

#label(defin:subspace-topology)
useenv defin [Subspace Topology] {
    Let $X$ be a topological space with the topology $\tau$, and $Y\subset X$ be
    nonempty set. Then one can consider the collection
    $$
        \tau' \defeq \{ Y\cap V : V \in \tau\}.
    $$
    Then $(Y,\tau')$ is a topological space.
    We say that $Y$ equipped the \it{subspace topology}\index{subspace topology}
    from $X$ meaning that the topology on $Y$ is $\tau'$.
}
useenv exec {
    Prove that the collection $\tau'$ in \Cref{defin:subspace-topology} is
    a topology on $Y$.
}

Let $X$ be a topological space and $Y$ be a subspace of $X$.
\Cref{defin:subspace-topology} means that every open set in $Y$ is the type
$U\cap Y$ where $U$ is open in $X$.
How about closed sets? One can easily infer that there is an analogous one for
closed sets.

#label(prop:subspace-topology-closed)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $Y$ a subspace of $X$.
    Then $E \subset Y$ is closed if and only if there exists a closed set $F$ in
    $X$ such that $E = F \cap Y$.
    \proof
    If $E$ is closed in $Y$, $Y -! E$ is open in $Y$.
    Thus by the definition of subspace topology, there exists an open set $U$ in
    $X$ such that $Y -! E = U \cap Y$. Then we have
    useenv align* {
        E = Y -! (Y -! E) = Y -! (U\cap Y) &=
        Y \cap [X -! (U\cap Y)] \\ &=
        Y \cap [(X -! U)\cup (X -! Y)] =
        Y \cap (X -! U).
    }
    Thus one side of the proposition is proved because $X-!U$ is closed in $X$.

    On the other hand, fix $E\subset Y$ and assume that there exists a
    closed set $F$ in $X$ such that $E = F\cap Y$.
    Then consider $Y -! E = Y -! (F\cap Y)$. By the upper calculation, one can
    easily check that $Y -! (F\cap Y) = Y \cap (X -! F)$.
    Since $X -! F$ is open in $X$, by the definition of the subspace topology,
    the set $Y -! E$ is open in $Y$, hence $E$ is closed in $Y$. \proved
}

Now consider that $X$ is a totally ordered set with an order $<=$.
In this case, we can give a topology on $X$ using its order.

#label(defin:order-space)
useenv defin [Order Space] {
    Let $X$ be a totally ordered set with an order $<=$.
    We equipped the topology on $X$ generated by a subbasis consisting
    all rays $\{y : x < y\}$ and $\{z : z < x\}$ for every $x\in X$.

    An \it{order space}\index{order space} is a totally ordered set equipped the
    topology described in above.
}

For instance, the standard topology for the real line $\R$ is the order
topology, meaning that the standard topology for $\R$ is generated by the collection
\[
    \{(a,b) : -oo <= a <= b <= oo\}.
\]

If $\{X_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ is a collection of topological spaces, we have
defined their product $X=\prod_{\alpha\in A}X_\alpha$ but yet we do not know
what kind of topology it has naturally. For each $\alpha\in A$ consider the
function $\pi_\alpha : X -> X_\alpha$ defined by $\pi_\alpha(x) =
x(\alpha)$.

useenv defin [Product topology] {
    Let $A$ be a nonempty set and $X_\alpha$ are topological space for each
    $\alpha\in A$. We define a \it{product topology}\index{product topology} on
    $X = \prod_{\alpha\in A}X_\alpha$ generated by a subbasis
    $$
        \{ \{\pi_\alpha \in V\} : \alpha\in A,\ V #"is open in"# X_\alpha\}.
    $$

    Recall that if $A = \{\alpha_j\}_{j=1}^n$ is finite, we identify
    $\prod_{j=1}^n X_{\alpha_j}$ and $X_{\alpha_1}\times ... \times
    X_{\alpha_n}$ with the canonical bijective map.
}

